entIds":["alignment:ab0f6c0e9c4e83915c96d494"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C (480 arrangements)","reason":"There are two gender patterns and 5! arrangements of the men. For fixed men, each wife has two permitted seats; the allowed-seat graph is a 10-cycle with exactly two perfect matchings. Thus the total is 2×5!×2=480.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_h234"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2008_AMC_10B_Q21","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_h231","cnm_h234"],"id":"forced-wife-seats","label":"Fix the men and force the remaining seats","methodDirectNodeIds":{"forced-wife-seats":["cnm_h235","math:finite-constraint-deduction"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h235","math:finite-constraint-deduction","cnm_h231","cnm_h234"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"alignmentIds":["alignment:ab0f6c0e9c4e83915c96d494"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C, 28","reason":"If Alice occupies an endpoint, there are 8 valid arrangements per endpoint, giving 16. Positions 2 and 4 each allow 4 arrangements, giving 8 more. The middle position allows 4. The total is 16+8+4=28.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_h234"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2017_AMC_12A_Q14","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"exclude-adjacency-blocks","label":"Inclusion-exclusion with adjacency blocks","methodDirectNodeIds":{"exclude-adjacency-blocks":["math:inclusion-exclusion-general","cnm_h234"]},"nodeIds":["math:inclusion-exclusion-general","cnm_h234"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"alignmentIds":["alignment:ab0f6c0e9c4e83915c96d494"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"E: 24","reason":"The valid period sets are {1,3,5}, {1,3,6}, {1,4,6}, and {2,4,6}. Each admits 3! = 6 assignments of the named courses, giving 4 × 6 = 24.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_h234"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2018_AMC_10A_Q04","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_h232"],"id":"enumerate-period-triples","label":"List valid periods, then order courses","methodDirectNodeIds":{"enumerate-period-triples":["cnm_h235","cnm_h231","cnm_h234"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h232","cnm_h234","cnm_h235","cnm_h231"]},{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"choose-gaps","label":"Choose gaps between other periods","methodDirectNodeIds":{"choose-gaps":["cnm_h239","cnm_h234","cnm_h231"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h239","cnm_h234","cnm_h231"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"alignmentIds":["alignment:ab0f6c0e9c4e83915c96d494"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D, 34","reason":"For prime n, the numerator has n-adic valuation n−1, while the denominator has valuation n, so it fails. The value n=4 also fails because v₂(15!)=11<12. Every composite n≥6 passes: for p^a exactly dividing n, the a one-unit deficits in the factorial valuation comparison are offset at the p^(a+1) term, since n/p≥a+1; the remaining differences are nonnegative. Including n=1, the count is 50−15 primes−1 exceptional composite=34.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_h234"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2019_AMC_10A_Q25","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"factorial-divisibility-and-grouping","label":"Prime factors and equal groups","methodDirectNodeIds":{"factorial-divisibility-and-grouping":["math:prime-valuations-factorials","math:double-counting","cnm_p157","cnm_h234"]},"nodeIds":["math:prime-valuations-factorials","math:double-counting","cnm_p157","cnm_h234"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"alignmentIds":["alignment:ab0f6c0e9c4e83915c96d494"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D (34)","reason":"Every prime p fails because v_p((p²-1)!)=p-1<p=v_p((p!)^p). Also, n=4 fails since the respective powers of 2 are 11 and 12. The ratio equals ((n-1)!/n) times the product of C(kn+n-1,n-1) for k=0,...,n-1. For every composite n>4, n divides (n-1)!, so this is integral; n=1 also works. Excluding the 15 primes and 4 leaves 50-15-1=34.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_h234"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2019_AMC_12A_Q24","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"factorial-partition-count","label":"Prove divisibility by counting groups","methodDirectNodeIds":{"factorial-partition-count":["math:prime-valuations-factorials","math:double-counting","cnm_p157","cnm_h234"]},"nodeIds":["math:prime-valuations-factorials","math:double-counting","cnm_p157","cnm_h234"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"alignmentIds":["alignment:ab0f6c0e9c4e83915c96d494"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B: 1/5","reason":"Every sequence with two draws of each color has probability (1·2·1·2)/(2·3·4·5)=1/30. There are C(4,2)=6 such sequences, giving 6/30=1/5.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_h234"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2020_AMC_10B_Q18","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"random-card-insertion","label":"Model the urn with card insertions","methodDirectNodeIds":{"random-card-insertion":["cnm_h245","cnm_h137","cnm_h234","math:bijective-counting"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h245","cnm_h137","cnm_h234","math:bijective-counting"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"alignmentIds":["alignment:ab0f6c0e9c4e83915c96d494"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B: 1/5","reason":"Ending with three balls of each color requires exactly two red and two blue draws. Every specified order of those draws has probability (1·2·1·2)/(2·3·4·5) = 1/30. There are C(4,2) = 6 such orders, so the probability is 6/30 = 1/5.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_h234"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2020_AMC_12B_Q16","reviewStatus":"machine_sco